# Trivy findings that are deliberate here, with the reason recorded in the repo
# rather than dismissed in the GitHub UI where a reviewer cannot see it.
#
# Re-examine both of these if the daemons ever stop needing root, or if an image
# in this repository starts serving something that is not a filesystem.

# DS-0002 — "Specify at least 1 USER command in Dockerfile".
#
# Both daemons require root and cannot be made to run as anything else:
# etcfuse mounts a FUSE filesystem with allow_other and default_permissions,
# which the kernel refuses to a non-root process unless user_allow_other is set
# in /etc/fuse.conf; and etcfuse-meta opens the shared block device O_DIRECT and
# issues the NVMe reservation commands the fencing protocol depends on. A USER
# line would produce an image that cannot mount, not a safer one. The
# deployment manifests are where this is contained, by giving the pods only the
# capabilities those two things need.
DS-0002

# DS-0026 — "Add HEALTHCHECK instruction in your Dockerfile".
#
# Liveness for these is decided outside the image and has to be: a node is
# healthy when its etcd lease is still being renewed and its mount still
# answers, and neither is visible from inside the container. The self-fencing
# watchdog is what acts on the first, and it exits the process rather than
# reporting a status. A HEALTHCHECK would give the container runtime a second,
# weaker opinion about whether a node should be restarted — and restarting a
# node that still holds inode locks is what the fencing protocol exists to make
# safe, not something to trigger on a missed probe.
DS-0026
